{"reason": "The baseline execution used only 10M rows and completed in 2 minutes with an RMSE of 3.47046, severely under-utilizing the available 64-core and 1 TB RAM compute while leaving TLC regulatory rules unmodeled. Modifying data_processing_and_feature_engineering directly targets the root cause of large pricing residuals by introducing discrete toll crossing flags, JFK-Manhattan flat-rate indicators, decomposed rotated street/avenue vectors, and non-linear distance transforms. Modifying training_evaluation scales data ingestion to 25M rows and deepens LightGBM tree capacity to 255 leaves with 2,500 estimators, enabling the booster to partition fine-grained spatial micro-routes without underfitting.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Scale data loading to 25,000,000 rows and enrich extract_features with domain-specific pricing logic: explicit JFK-to-Manhattan flat-fare flags, East/Hudson River water crossing and toll plaza proximity indicators, separate avenue and street components of the 29-degree rotated Manhattan grid, and non-linear distance transforms (log1p, square-root, and tortuosity ratio). WHY: NYC taxi fares feature discrete non-Euclidean TLC regulations including airport flat rates and tunnel/bridge tolls, while avenue traffic moves significantly faster than crosstown street transit. HOW: Expand the bounding and feature logic inside extract_features to compute directional JFK-Manhattan flags, bridge/tunnel toll distances, avenue versus street distances, and distance ratio features while loading 25M rows using train_usecols and memory-efficient datatypes. INTERFACE: Keep the function signature extract_features(df: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame intact and preserve all original feature column names and types alongside the new features.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Expand the LightGBM model capacity to num_leaves=255, decrease learning_rate to 0.05, increase n_estimators to 2500 with 50 rounds early stopping, and set min_child_samples to 100 for stability on the 25M-sample training set. WHY: The baseline completed in only 2 minutes on 64 CPU cores, indicating that the 127-leaf model severely underfit the complex spatial geometry and cross-borough pricing boundaries of New York City. HOW: Update model_params in the training pipeline, fit LGBMRegressor on the scaled 25M train split with validation monitoring every 200 rounds, persist best_lgbm_model.joblib, clip predictions to [2.50, 500.0], and output submission.csv. INTERFACE: Retain random_state=42, 95/5 train/val split ratio, RMSE evaluation metric, clipping bounds [2.50, 500.0], and submission output at ./submission/submission.csv matching key and fare_amount schema."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
